  "content": {
    "id": "p53-q21-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "21",
    "prompt": "Consider the curve $y=x\\log_e x-x$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p53-q21-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write down the domain and $x$-intercept.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x>0$; $x$-intercept $(e,0)$.",
          "worked": "The logarithm requires $x>0$.\n\nFor the $x$-intercept:\n$$\\begin{aligned}x\\ln x-x&=0\\\\x(\\ln x-1)&=0\\\\\\ln x&=1\\\\x&=e\\end{aligned}$$\nSince $x>0$, the intercept is $(e,0)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q21-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $y'=\\log_e x$ and find $y''$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=\\log_e x$, $y''=\\dfrac1x$.",
          "worked": "Using the product rule:\n$$\\begin{aligned}y'&=1\\cdot\\ln x+x\\cdot\\frac1x-1\\\\y'&=\\ln x+1-1\\\\y'&=\\ln x\\\\y''&=\\frac1x\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q21-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence show that there is one stationary point and determine its nature.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(1,-1)$ is the only stationary point and is a minimum.",
          "worked": {
