      {
        "id": "p53-q21-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "What does $y''$ tell you about the curve?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y''>0$, so the curve is concave up throughout its domain.",
          "worked": "For every $x>0$, $y''=\\dfrac1x>0$. Therefore the curve is concave up throughout its domain and has no points of inflection.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q21-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Given that $y\\to0^-$ as $x\\to0^+$, and that the tangent approaches vertical as $x\\to0^+$, sketch the curve and write down its range.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Range: $y\\ge-1$. The sketch has an open endpoint at $(0,0)$, minimum $(1,-1)$ and $x$-intercept $(e,0)$; it is concave up throughout.",
          "worked": "Approach the excluded origin from below with the tangent approaching vertical. The curve decreases to $(1,-1)$, then increases through $(e,0)$ and continues upwards without bound. It is concave up throughout $x>0$.\n\nThe minimum value is $-1$, and $y\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$, so the range is $[-1,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
